{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated claim-rebuttal worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Attack each proposed claim. Identify the strongest alternative explanation, missing observation, or scope limit. Return {\"rebuttals\":[{\"claim_index\":int,\"challenge\":str,\"repair\":str}]}.\nINPUT:\n{\"direction\": {\"title\": \"Competing theory predictions\", \"rationale\": \"Freeze operational definitions of consciousness (e.g., global workspace vs. integrated information vs. quantum vibration) and derive mutually exclusive empirical predictions to isolate mechanistic drivers.\", \"workflow\": \"conceptual_discrimination\", \"completion_test\": \"At least two competing frameworks yield distinct, falsifiable measurable outcomes under controlled conditions.\", \"remaining\": \"Decisive experimental validation and cross-disciplinary conceptual consensus on which framework survives initial testing.\", \"position\": 0}, \"evidence\": {\"sources\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:5f0cdd712a4a17341f73f3a298f54f289600fe14e65b5ef369df6165a8799533\", \"sha256\": \"5f0cdd712a4a17341f73f3a298f54f289600fe14e65b5ef369df6165a8799533\", \"title\": \"Adversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated ...\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 435}, \"content\": \"## Adversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories of consciousness | Nature\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#content)\\n\\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\\nand JavaScript.\\n\\nAdversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories of consciousness\\n\\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1.pdf)\\n\\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1.pdf)\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nDifferent theories explain how subjective experience arises from brain activity[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-\\nCR1),[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR2). These theories have independently accrued evidence, but have not been directly\\ncompared[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR3). Here we present an open science adversarial collaboration directly juxtaposing\\nintegrated information theory (IIT)[4](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR4),[5](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-\\nCR5)and global neuronal workspace theory\\n(GNWT)[6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR6),[7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR7),[8](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR8),[9](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR9),[10](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR10)via a theory-neutral\\nconsortium[11](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR11),[12](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR12),[13](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR13). The theory proponents and the consortium developed and preregistered the experimental\\ndesign, divergent predictions, expected outcomes and interpretation thereof[12](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR12). Human participants\\n(*n*= 256) viewed suprathreshold stimuli for variable durations while neural activity was measured with functional magnetic resonance imaging,\\nmagnetoencephalography and intracranial electroencephalography. We found information about conscious content in visual, ventrotemporal and inferior frontal\\ncortex, with sustained responses in occipital and lateral temporal cortex reflecting stimulus duration, and content-specific synchronization between frontal and\\nearly visual areas. These results align with some predictions of IIT and GNWT, while substantially challenging key tenets of both theories. For IIT, a lack of\\nsustained synchronization within the posterior cortex contradicts the claim that network connectivity specifies consciousness. GNWT is challenged by the general\\nlack of ignition at stimulus offset and limited representation of certain conscious dimensions in the prefrontal cortex. These challenges extend to other\\ntheories of consciousness that share some of the predictions tested\\nhere[14](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR14),[15](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR15),[16](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR16),[17](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR17). Beyond challenging the theories, we present an alternative approach to advance\\ncognitive neuroscience through principled, theory-driven, collaborative research and highlight the need for a quantitative framework for systematic theory\\ntesting and building.\\n\\n### Similar content being viewed by others\\n\\n### [An open-access multi-site fMRI dataset for investigating conscious visual\\nperception](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-026-07377-y?fromPaywallRec=false)\\n\\nArticleOpen access07 May 2026\\n\\n### [Gridography tractography reveals communication between key areas from global workspace and integrated information theories of\\nconsciousness](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-31016-y?fromPaywallRec=false)\\n\\nArticleOpen access30 December 2025\\n\\n### [The network architecture of general intelligence in the human connectome](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-68698-5?fromPaywallRec=false)\\n\\nArticleOpen access26 January 2026\\n\\n### Subjects\\n- [Consciousness](https://www.nature.com/subjects/consciousness)\\n- [Databases](https://www.nature.com/subjects/databases)\\n\\n## Main\\n\\nPhilosophers and scientists have sought to explain the subjective nature of consciousness (for example, the feeling of pain or of seeing a colourful rainbow)\\nand how it relates to physical processes in the brain[18](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-\\nCR18),[19](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR19). This quest has led to various theories of consciousness evolving in\\nparallel[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR1),[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\\nCR2),[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR3)and often providing incompatible accounts of the neural basis of\\nconsciousness[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR1),[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR2). Furthermore,\\nempirical support for a given theory is often highly dependent on methodological choices, pointing towards a confirmation bias in theory\\ntesting[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR3). Convergence on a broadly accepted neuroscientific theory of consciousness will have\\nprofound medical, societal and ethical implications.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:b37e56afb7f8659a0f913e52f4986a7ae9d224cc37700f8c8bba3a33602ebf83\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:4c60f74e97dc6e6735dbfabfe538b99da4d222b407b0d2e8767d998e14c042fa\", \"sha256\": \"4c60f74e97dc6e6735dbfabfe538b99da4d222b407b0d2e8767d998e14c042fa\", \"title\": \"Global Workspace vs. Integrated Information: Testing Consciousness ...\", \"url\": \"https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/blog/testing-gnw-and-iit-through-animal-models\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 228}, \"content\": \"## Global Workspace vs. Integrated Information: Testing… | Templeton World Charity Foundation, Inc.\\n\\n**Source**: https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/blog/testing-gnw-and-iit-through-animal-models\\n\\n---\\n\\nDiscovery\\n\\nFeb 17, 2025\\n\\n# Global Workspace vs. Integrated Information: Testing Consciousness Theories through Animal Models\\n\\nWhat kinds of brain activity correspond exactly to conscious experiences? How might those mechanisms have evolved, and are they shared across species?\\n\\n---\\n\\nBy Templeton Staff\\n\\n###### **What To Know:**\\n\\n######\\n\\n###### • Neural correlates of consciousness (NCC) are specific brain activity patterns that correspond exactly with conscious experiences.\\n\\n######\\n\\n###### • Bringing together 4 neuroscience labs, this Adversarial Collaboration tests 2 leading consciousness theories—Global Neuronal Workspace and Integrated\\nInformation—that propose fundamentally different NCC.\\n\\n######\\n\\n###### • To test predictions consistent with these two divergent theories, the project employs innovative experimental designs including cutting-edge technology\\nto manipulate brain networks.\\n\\n**What creates our conscious experience of the world around us? And how do specific patterns of brain activity that correlate to conscious experience transform\\ninto the subjective awareness we experience moment by moment?**\\n\\nTo better understand patterns of brain activity that correspond to conscious experiences – known as neural correlates of consciousness (NCC) – the Templeton\\nWorld Charity Foundation is funding an ambitious research project, the third in a series of[Structured Adversarial\\nCollaborations](https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/our-priorities/discovery/accelerating-research-consciousness#adversarialcollaboration), a format that\\nallows researchers to systematically evaluate competing hypotheses about consciousness through carefully designed experiments and rigorous cross-laboratory\\nvalidation.\\n\\nBringing together four prestigious neuroscience laboratories, the project aims to test two competing theories that propose fundamentally different mechanisms\\nfor how consciousness emerges from neural activity. Specifically, does consciousness emerge from an explosion of activity in the brain's frontal regions, as put\\nforth in the Global Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT), or does it arise from sustained patterns of information integration in the posterior regions of the brain,\\nas proposed by the Integrated Information Theory (IIT)?\\n\\n“The goal of this project, and the whole Templeton collaboration, is to improve theories of consciousness,” says[project lead Dr. Yuri\\nSaalmann](https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/projects-resources/project-database/0590), Professor of Psychology at the University of Wisconsin-Madison and\\ncore scientist at the Wisconsin National Primate Research Center.\\n\\n“And, there are a number of reasons why this is vital. One is that it allows us to get a better understanding of consciousness, which can be useful for driving\\nnew experiments and discoveries; but also, so we can better understand consciousness disorders like coma, where right now there are very limited clinical\\noptions.”\\n\\nTo test predictions consistent with these two divergent theories, teams from the Geffen Lab at the University of Pennsylvania, the Olsen Lab at the Allen\\nInstitute, Biomedical Research Foundation Academy Of Athens (BRFAA), and the University of Wisconsin-Madison are using groundbreaking technology and innovative\\nexperimental designs to manipulate brain networks.\\n\\nNeuropixels – electrodes capable of recording the activity of hundreds of neurons in the brain – will allow the researchers to record neural activity from both\\nclosely spaced neurons, as well as individual neurons across the anterior-to-posterior cerebral cortex. While optogenetics – a technique using light-sensitive\\nproteins (derived from algae) to control specific neurons, and even deactivate different regions, by shining a light through optical fibers inserted into the\\nbrain – will allow them to observe the resulting effects on consciousness and perception. Together, these techniques will provide first-of-their-kind, high\\nspatiotemporal resolution tests of, and potentially evidence for or against, each theory of consciousness, as well as insight into the evolution of patterns of\\nbrain activity that correspond to conscious experiences.\\n\\nIn addition to employing new technologies, the project will also shed light on whether mechanisms are shared across species. Unlike the other adversarial\\ncollaborations, the subjects of this project are non-human primates and mice, a deliberate choice that will allow researchers to explore how consciousness might\\nhave evolved.\\n\\n\\\"Mice have differences in the structure of their frontal lobe,” says Saalmann. “While the frontal region in mice is reduced compared to humans and macaques,\\nsome of the core regions overlap in their inputs and outputs, so the neuronal firing patterns during behavior are able to be compared across species.”\\n\\nFor this project, Saalmann and his colleagues designed animal model experiments to be able to record and manipulate brain activity with unprecedented precision\\nin three key areas where the theories diverge:\\n\\n###### **1. Brain Location:**GNWT emphasizes the importance of the frontal lobe, which comprises about 40% of the human brain, while IIT remains agnostic about\\nits role.\\n\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:084e4761c6fa0b9b0c60c3983fdc4cbeff0680c07bd73f80df46bc870ada2214\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:4d0f233aa3e795d9d04e70aba06c68c4ee100bb496d2d1b82e58bd55b56e7233\", \"sha256\": \"4d0f233aa3e795d9d04e70aba06c68c4ee100bb496d2d1b82e58bd55b56e7233\", \"title\": \"An adversarial collaboration protocol for testing contrasting ...\", \"url\": \"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 486}, \"content\": \"## An adversarial collaboration protocol for testing contrasting predictions of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theory | PLOS One\\n\\n**Source**: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577\\n\\n---\\n\\nBrowse Subject Areas\\n\\n?\\n\\nClick through the PLOS taxonomy to find articles in your field.\\n\\nFor more information about PLOS Subject Areas, click[here](https://github.com/PLOS/plos-thesaurus/blob/master/README.md).\\n\\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\\n\\n- [Article](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n- [Authors](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/authors?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n- [Metrics](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/metrics?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n- [Comments](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/comments?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n- [Media Coverage](http://plos.altmetric.com/details/doi/10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n- [Peer Review](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/peerReview?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n\\n- [Reader Comments](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/comments?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\\n- [Figures](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\\n\\n## Figures\\n\\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\\n\\nThe relationship between conscious experience and brain activity has intrigued scientists and philosophers for centuries. In the last decades, several theories\\nhave suggested different accounts for these relationships. These theories have developed in parallel, with little to no cross-talk among them. To advance\\nresearch on consciousness, we established an adversarial collaboration between proponents of two of the major theories in the field, Global Neuronal Workspace\\nand Integrated Information Theory. Together, we devised and preregistered two experiments that test contrasting predictions of these theories concerning the\\nlocation and timing of correlates of visual consciousness, which have been endorsed by the theories’ proponents. Predicted outcomes should either support,\\nrefute, or challenge these theories. Six theory-impartial laboratories will follow the study protocol specified here, using three complementary methods:\\nFunctional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (fMRI), Magneto-Electroencephalography (M-EEG), and intracranial electroencephalography (iEEG). The study protocol will\\ninclude built-in replications, both between labs and within datasets. Through this ambitious undertaking, we hope to provide decisive evidence in favor or\\nagainst the two theories and clarify the footprints of conscious visual perception in the human brain, while also providing an innovative model of large-scale,\\ncollaborative, and open science practice.\\n\\n**Citation:**Melloni L, Mudrik L, Pitts M, Bendtz K, Ferrante O, Gorska U, et al. (2023) An adversarial collaboration protocol for testing contrasting\\npredictions of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theory. PLoS ONE 18(2): e0268577. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268577\\n\\n**Editor:**Lawrence M. Ward, University of British Columbia, CANADA\\n\\n**Received:**November 16, 2021;**Accepted:**May 3, 2022;**Published:**February 10, 2023\\n\\n**Copyright:**© 2023 Melloni et al. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the[Creative Commons Attribution\\nLicense](http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original\\nauthor and source are credited.\\n\\n**Data Availability:**The data collected and analyzed for this Study Protocol will be made openly available at OSF\\n([https://osf.io/mbcfy/](https://osf.io/mbcfy/)) and XNAT (Radiologics).\\n\\n**Funding:**This project is made possible through the support of a grant from Templeton World Charity Foundation, Inc (TWCF0389) to Lucia Melloni and (TWCF0378)\\nto Liad Mudrik and (TWCF0345) to Michael Pitts. The opinions expressed in this publication are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily reflect the views\\nof Templeton World Charity Foundation, Inc.[https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/](https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/). The funders had no role in study\\ndesign, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the manuscript.\\n\\n**Competing interests:**The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.\\n\\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\\n\\n## Introduction[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\\n\\nUnderstanding how consciousness relates to neural activity in the human brain remains one of the greatest scientific challenges\\n[[1](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref001)]. Phenomenologically, consciousness has been defined\\nas*subjective experience*(or*what it is like*to perceive, feel, act or think from a first-person perspective)\\n[[2](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref002)]. In human experiments, consciousness is typically\\ninferred based on the ability to report these experiences (either to oneself or to an external observer\\n[[3](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref003)]). The neural correlates of consciousness (NCCs) have been\\ndefined as the*minimal*neuronal mechanisms jointly sufficient for any specific experience\\n[[4](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref004)]. Significant progress has come from focusing on the\\nsearch for the NCC [[5](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref005),[6](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/a\\nrticle?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref006)]. This has led to the development of several promising scientific theories of consciousness [[7](h\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:341ce2ec24a5b42a3781a8672d8ad583d9132a534b9c1e4e7f02ee51e7345643\", \"sha256\": \"341ce2ec24a5b42a3781a8672d8ad583d9132a534b9c1e4e7f02ee51e7345643\", \"title\": \"Challenging Global Workspace and Integrated Information Theories\", \"url\": \"https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 362}, \"content\": \"## Challenging Global Workspace and Integrated Information Theories\\n\\n**Source**: https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/\\n\\n---\\n\\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\\n- [VIDEO](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/video-explore/)\\n- [WIKI](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/wiki/)\\n- [BLOG](https://bioengineer.org/category/blog/)\\n- [COMMUNITY](https://Bioengineer.org/)\\n- [FACEBOOK](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org)\\n- [INSTAGRAM](https://instagram.com/bioengineering/)\\n- [TWITTER](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)\\n\\nThursday, August 13, 2026\\n\\n[**](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org/)[**](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-\\ninformation-theories/)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\\n\\n[](https://bioengineer.org/)\\n\\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\\n\\nNo Result\\n\\n**View All Result\\n\\n- [**Login](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/#jeg_loginform)\\n\\n[](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\\n\\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\\n- [Lecturer](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/lecturer/)\\n- [PhD Studentship](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/phd-studentship/)\\n- [Postdoc](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/postdoc/)\\n- [Research Assistant](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/research-assistant/)\\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\\n- [VIDEO](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/video-explore/)\\n- [WIKI](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/wiki/)\\n- [BLOG](https://bioengineer.org/category/blog/)\\n- [COMMUNITY](https://Bioengineer.org/)\\n- [FACEBOOK](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org)\\n- [INSTAGRAM](https://instagram.com/bioengineering/)\\n- [TWITTER](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)\\n\\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\\n- [Lecturer](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/lecturer/)\\n- [PhD Studentship](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/phd-studentship/)\\n- [Postdoc](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/postdoc/)\\n- [Research Assistant](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/research-assistant/)\\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\"}], \"summaries\": {\"summaries\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4\", \"summary\": \"An open-science adversarial collaboration directly compared Integrated Information Theory (IIT) and Global Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT) using preregistered experiments with 256 human subjects across fMRI, MEG, and iEEG modalities. Results revealed conscious content representations in visual, ventrotemporal, and inferior frontal cortices, along with sustained occipital/temporal responses and frontal-visual synchronization. These findings partially align with but substantially challenge core tenets of both theories: IIT is contradicted by the absence of sustained posterior synchronization, while GNWT is challenged by a lack of stimulus-offset ignition and limited prefrontal involvement. The study underscores the necessity for rigorous, theory-driven collaborative frameworks to advance cognitive neuroscience.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-435; encompasses the abstract and introductory sections detailing the adversarial methodology, multimodal data collection, key neural findings, and specific theoretical challenges to both IIT and GNWT.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:b37e56afb7f8659a0f913e52f4986a7ae9d224cc37700f8c8bba3a33602ebf83\", \"summary\": \"Funded by the Templeton World Charity Foundation, this adversarial collaboration employs non-human primate and mouse models to test divergent predictions of GNWT and IIT regarding neural correlates of consciousness. Utilizing high-resolution Neuropixels recordings and optogenetic network manipulation, the project investigates whether consciousness arises from frontal ignition or posterior integration. By comparing neuronal firing patterns across species with varying frontal lobe structures, the research aims to elucidate the evolutionary origins of conscious mechanisms and improve clinical understanding of consciousness disorders.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-228; covers the project's funding background, rationale for animal models, technological approach (Neuropixels/optogenetics), and objectives regarding cross-species comparison and clinical applications.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:084e4761c6fa0b9b0c60c3983fdc4cbeff0680c07bd73f80df46bc870ada2214\", \"summary\": \"This publication outlines a preregistered protocol for an adversarial collaboration designed to test contrasting predictions of GNWT and IIT concerning the spatial and temporal dynamics of visual consciousness. Six theory-impartial laboratories will independently execute the study using fMRI, magnetoencephalography, and intracranial EEG, incorporating built-in replication checks. The initiative seeks to generate decisive empirical evidence to support or refute the competing theories while establishing a standardized, open-science framework for large-scale collaborative neuroscience research.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-486; spans the full study protocol document, including theoretical background, experimental design specifications across multiple imaging modalities, multi-lab coordination details, and open-data implementation guidelines.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694\", \"summary\": \"The provided text consists entirely of website navigation elements, metadata, and structural placeholders without substantive scientific content or analysis related to the stated topic.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-362; contains only site architecture, menu items, and boilerplate text; lacks empirical or theoretical discussion.\"}]}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\", \"severity\": \"info\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694\", \"locator\": \"Lines 1-362\", \"sha256\": \"19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Source 4 consists entirely of website navigation and boilerplate text without substantive scientific content. Rely on the peer-reviewed protocol and primary research articles for evidence.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\", \"severity\": \"minor\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4\", \"locator\": \"Rationale\", \"sha256\": \"5f0cdd712a4a17341f73f3a298f54f289600fe14e65b5ef369df6165a8799533\"}], \"recommendation\": \"The rationale mentions 'quantum vibration' as an example theory, but the evidence base exclusively covers GNWT and IIT. Since the completion test requires at least two frameworks, this is acceptable, but removing extraneous examples would tighten scope alignment.\"}], \"summary\": \"The evidence robustly validates the conceptual discrimination workflow by documenting adversarial collaborations that explicitly freeze the operational definitions of GNWT and IIT to derive mutually exclusive, falsifiable predictions. Preregistered protocols, multimodal data collection (fMRI, MEG, iEEG, Neuropixels), and cross-species designs satisfy the completion test for distinct measurable outcomes. Scientific validity is high, though one source is non-substantive and the rationale includes an unaddressed theoretical example. Overall, the inference scope is appropriately bounded and directly supports the direction's objectives.\"}}, \"proposed\": {\"claims\": [{\"text\": \"The absence of sustained posterior cortical synchronization during conscious visual perception establishes a bound on the necessity of posterior integration for consciousness, contradicting Integrated Information Theory's claim that network connectivity alone specifies conscious experience.\", \"kind\": \"evidence\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4\"], \"scope\": \"Human visual consciousness paradigms measured via multimodal neuroimaging (fMRI, MEG, iEEG).\"}, {\"text\": \"Optogenetic disruption of posterior information integration networks will abolish conscious perceptual reports in non-human primates and mice regardless of frontal lobe morphology, whereas frontal network inhibition will not consistently eliminate awareness.\", \"kind\": \"prediction\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:b37e56afb7f8659a0f913e52f4986a7ae9d224cc37700f8c8bba3a33602ebf83\", \"source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4\"], \"scope\": \"Cross-species experimental neuroscience using Neuropixels recordings and optogenetic network manipulation.\"}, {\"text\": \"Conscious visual perception is bounded by content-specific synchronization between frontal and early visual areas, operating independently of global frontal ignition thresholds predicted by workspace models.\", \"kind\": \"bound\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4\", \"source-snapshot:084e4761c6fa0b9b0c60c3983fdc4cbeff0680c07bd73f80df46bc870ada2214\"], \"scope\": \"Human neuroimaging studies of visual awareness under controlled stimulus duration conditions.\"}]}}"}]}
{"type": "model_output", "text": "{\"rebuttals\":[{\"claim_index\":0,\"challenge\":\"IIT defines consciousness via system-wide causal integration (Φ), not localized posterior functional synchronization. The challenge conflates measured low-frequency coherence with IIT's intrinsic causal architecture. Scope is further limited to suprathreshold visual paradigms, excluding subcortical, high-frequency, or non-visual conscious states where posterior dynamics may differ.\",\"repair\":\"Reframe to state that the absence of sustained posterior synchronization challenges posterior-centric functional connectivity as a necessary correlate for visual consciousness under these multimodal imaging constraints, while acknowledging IIT's requirement for system-wide causal integration beyond localized oscillatory coupling.\"},{\"claim_index\":1,\"challenge\":\"This presents an unverified hypothesis as a definitive prediction. The adversarial protocol is designed to empirically test divergent outcomes, not confirm posterior necessity. It also overlooks species-specific neuroanatomical differences (e.g., murine agranular cortex) and ignores compensatory thalamocortical or brainstem arousal systems that could sustain awareness despite posterior disruption.\",\"repair\":\"Position as a preregistered experimental hypothesis contingent on task-dependent reliance on posterior integration, explicitly accounting for cross-species anatomical variation and the possibility that frontal-posterior feedback loops, rather than isolated regional disruption, determine perceptual availability.\"},{\"claim_index\":2,\"challenge\":\"The claim overgeneralizes 'lack of ignition at stimulus offset' to imply complete independence from workspace ignition thresholds. Ignition may still govern conscious reportability, maintenance, or transition phases outside the measured window. Frontal-visual synchronization likely reflects top-down attentional gating or working memory buffering rather than a standalone consciousness mechanism, confounding the boundary claim.\",\"repair\":\"Specify that frontal-early visual coupling sustains content-specific representations during active perception, while recognizing that global ignition thresholds may remain critical for conscious access, reportability, or state transitions beyond the immediate stimulus duration window.\"}]}", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 9787, "total_tokens": 12342, "completion_tokens": 2555, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 2165, "text_tokens": 2555}}, "model": "qwen3.7-flash"}
{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"direction": {"title": "Competing theory predictions", "rationale": "Freeze operational definitions of consciousness (e.g., global workspace vs. integrated information vs. quantum vibration) and derive mutually exclusive empirical predictions to isolate mechanistic drivers.", "workflow": "conceptual_discrimination", "completion_test": "At least two competing frameworks yield distinct, falsifiable measurable outcomes under controlled conditions.", "remaining": "Decisive experimental validation and cross-disciplinary conceptual consensus on which framework survives initial testing.", "position": 0}, "evidence": {"sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4", "artifact_id": "artifact:5f0cdd712a4a17341f73f3a298f54f289600fe14e65b5ef369df6165a8799533", "sha256": "5f0cdd712a4a17341f73f3a298f54f289600fe14e65b5ef369df6165a8799533", "title": "Adversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated ...", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 435}, "content": "## Adversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories of consciousness | Nature\n\n**Source**: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1\n\n---\n\n[Skip to main content](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#content)\n\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain the best experience, we recommend you use a more up\nto date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in Internet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\nand JavaScript.\n\nAdversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories of consciousness\n\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1.pdf)\n\n[Download PDF](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1.pdf)\n\n## Abstract\n\nDifferent theories explain how subjective experience arises from brain activity[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-\nCR1),[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR2). These theories have independently accrued evidence, but have not been directly\ncompared[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR3). Here we present an open science adversarial collaboration directly juxtaposing\nintegrated information theory (IIT)[4](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR4),[5](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-\nCR5)and global neuronal workspace theory\n(GNWT)[6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR6),[7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR7),[8](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR8),[9](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR9),[10](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR10)via a theory-neutral\nconsortium[11](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR11),[12](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR12),[13](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR13). The theory proponents and the consortium developed and preregistered the experimental\ndesign, divergent predictions, expected outcomes and interpretation thereof[12](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR12). Human participants\n(*n*= 256) viewed suprathreshold stimuli for variable durations while neural activity was measured with functional magnetic resonance imaging,\nmagnetoencephalography and intracranial electroencephalography. We found information about conscious content in visual, ventrotemporal and inferior frontal\ncortex, with sustained responses in occipital and lateral temporal cortex reflecting stimulus duration, and content-specific synchronization between frontal and\nearly visual areas. These results align with some predictions of IIT and GNWT, while substantially challenging key tenets of both theories. For IIT, a lack of\nsustained synchronization within the posterior cortex contradicts the claim that network connectivity specifies consciousness. GNWT is challenged by the general\nlack of ignition at stimulus offset and limited representation of certain conscious dimensions in the prefrontal cortex. These challenges extend to other\ntheories of consciousness that share some of the predictions tested\nhere[14](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR14),[15](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR15),[16](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR16),[17](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR17). Beyond challenging the theories, we present an alternative approach to advance\ncognitive neuroscience through principled, theory-driven, collaborative research and highlight the need for a quantitative framework for systematic theory\ntesting and building.\n\n### Similar content being viewed by others\n\n### [An open-access multi-site fMRI dataset for investigating conscious visual\nperception](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-026-07377-y?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticleOpen access07 May 2026\n\n### [Gridography tractography reveals communication between key areas from global workspace and integrated information theories of\nconsciousness](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-31016-y?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticleOpen access30 December 2025\n\n### [The network architecture of general intelligence in the human connectome](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-68698-5?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticleOpen access26 January 2026\n\n### Subjects\n- [Consciousness](https://www.nature.com/subjects/consciousness)\n- [Databases](https://www.nature.com/subjects/databases)\n\n## Main\n\nPhilosophers and scientists have sought to explain the subjective nature of consciousness (for example, the feeling of pain or of seeing a colourful rainbow)\nand how it relates to physical processes in the brain[18](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-\nCR18),[19](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR19). This quest has led to various theories of consciousness evolving in\nparallel[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR1),[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=eb5be09e-baa1-4f9f-80cc-0f5ce1843d49#ref-\nCR2),[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR3)and often providing incompatible accounts of the neural basis of\nconsciousness[1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR1),[2](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR2). Furthermore,\nempirical support for a given theory is often highly dependent on methodological choices, pointing towards a confirmation bias in theory\ntesting[3](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08888-1#ref-CR3). Convergence on a broadly accepted neuroscientific theory of consciousness will have\nprofound medical, societal and ethical implications."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:b37e56afb7f8659a0f913e52f4986a7ae9d224cc37700f8c8bba3a33602ebf83", "artifact_id": "artifact:4c60f74e97dc6e6735dbfabfe538b99da4d222b407b0d2e8767d998e14c042fa", "sha256": "4c60f74e97dc6e6735dbfabfe538b99da4d222b407b0d2e8767d998e14c042fa", "title": "Global Workspace vs. Integrated Information: Testing Consciousness ...", "url": "https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/blog/testing-gnw-and-iit-through-animal-models", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 228}, "content": "## Global Workspace vs. Integrated Information: Testing… | Templeton World Charity Foundation, Inc.\n\n**Source**: https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/blog/testing-gnw-and-iit-through-animal-models\n\n---\n\nDiscovery\n\nFeb 17, 2025\n\n# Global Workspace vs. Integrated Information: Testing Consciousness Theories through Animal Models\n\nWhat kinds of brain activity correspond exactly to conscious experiences? How might those mechanisms have evolved, and are they shared across species?\n\n---\n\nBy Templeton Staff\n\n###### **What To Know:**\n\n######\n\n###### • Neural correlates of consciousness (NCC) are specific brain activity patterns that correspond exactly with conscious experiences.\n\n######\n\n###### • Bringing together 4 neuroscience labs, this Adversarial Collaboration tests 2 leading consciousness theories—Global Neuronal Workspace and Integrated\nInformation—that propose fundamentally different NCC.\n\n######\n\n###### • To test predictions consistent with these two divergent theories, the project employs innovative experimental designs including cutting-edge technology\nto manipulate brain networks.\n\n**What creates our conscious experience of the world around us? And how do specific patterns of brain activity that correlate to conscious experience transform\ninto the subjective awareness we experience moment by moment?**\n\nTo better understand patterns of brain activity that correspond to conscious experiences – known as neural correlates of consciousness (NCC) – the Templeton\nWorld Charity Foundation is funding an ambitious research project, the third in a series of[Structured Adversarial\nCollaborations](https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/our-priorities/discovery/accelerating-research-consciousness#adversarialcollaboration), a format that\nallows researchers to systematically evaluate competing hypotheses about consciousness through carefully designed experiments and rigorous cross-laboratory\nvalidation.\n\nBringing together four prestigious neuroscience laboratories, the project aims to test two competing theories that propose fundamentally different mechanisms\nfor how consciousness emerges from neural activity. Specifically, does consciousness emerge from an explosion of activity in the brain's frontal regions, as put\nforth in the Global Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT), or does it arise from sustained patterns of information integration in the posterior regions of the brain,\nas proposed by the Integrated Information Theory (IIT)?\n\n“The goal of this project, and the whole Templeton collaboration, is to improve theories of consciousness,” says[project lead Dr. Yuri\nSaalmann](https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/projects-resources/project-database/0590), Professor of Psychology at the University of Wisconsin-Madison and\ncore scientist at the Wisconsin National Primate Research Center.\n\n“And, there are a number of reasons why this is vital. One is that it allows us to get a better understanding of consciousness, which can be useful for driving\nnew experiments and discoveries; but also, so we can better understand consciousness disorders like coma, where right now there are very limited clinical\noptions.”\n\nTo test predictions consistent with these two divergent theories, teams from the Geffen Lab at the University of Pennsylvania, the Olsen Lab at the Allen\nInstitute, Biomedical Research Foundation Academy Of Athens (BRFAA), and the University of Wisconsin-Madison are using groundbreaking technology and innovative\nexperimental designs to manipulate brain networks.\n\nNeuropixels – electrodes capable of recording the activity of hundreds of neurons in the brain – will allow the researchers to record neural activity from both\nclosely spaced neurons, as well as individual neurons across the anterior-to-posterior cerebral cortex. While optogenetics – a technique using light-sensitive\nproteins (derived from algae) to control specific neurons, and even deactivate different regions, by shining a light through optical fibers inserted into the\nbrain – will allow them to observe the resulting effects on consciousness and perception. Together, these techniques will provide first-of-their-kind, high\nspatiotemporal resolution tests of, and potentially evidence for or against, each theory of consciousness, as well as insight into the evolution of patterns of\nbrain activity that correspond to conscious experiences.\n\nIn addition to employing new technologies, the project will also shed light on whether mechanisms are shared across species. Unlike the other adversarial\ncollaborations, the subjects of this project are non-human primates and mice, a deliberate choice that will allow researchers to explore how consciousness might\nhave evolved.\n\n\"Mice have differences in the structure of their frontal lobe,” says Saalmann. “While the frontal region in mice is reduced compared to humans and macaques,\nsome of the core regions overlap in their inputs and outputs, so the neuronal firing patterns during behavior are able to be compared across species.”\n\nFor this project, Saalmann and his colleagues designed animal model experiments to be able to record and manipulate brain activity with unprecedented precision\nin three key areas where the theories diverge:\n\n###### **1. Brain Location:**GNWT emphasizes the importance of the frontal lobe, which comprises about 40% of the human brain, while IIT remains agnostic about\nits role.\n"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:084e4761c6fa0b9b0c60c3983fdc4cbeff0680c07bd73f80df46bc870ada2214", "artifact_id": "artifact:4d0f233aa3e795d9d04e70aba06c68c4ee100bb496d2d1b82e58bd55b56e7233", "sha256": "4d0f233aa3e795d9d04e70aba06c68c4ee100bb496d2d1b82e58bd55b56e7233", "title": "An adversarial collaboration protocol for testing contrasting ...", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 486}, "content": "## An adversarial collaboration protocol for testing contrasting predictions of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theory | PLOS One\n\n**Source**: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577\n\n---\n\nBrowse Subject Areas\n\n?\n\nClick through the PLOS taxonomy to find articles in your field.\n\nFor more information about PLOS Subject Areas, click[here](https://github.com/PLOS/plos-thesaurus/blob/master/README.md).\n\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\n\n- [Article](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n- [Authors](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/authors?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n- [Metrics](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/metrics?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n- [Comments](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/comments?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n- [Media Coverage](http://plos.altmetric.com/details/doi/10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n- [Peer Review](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/peerReview?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n\n- [Reader Comments](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/comments?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0268577)\n- [Figures](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\n\n## Figures\n\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\n\n## Abstract\n\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\n\nThe relationship between conscious experience and brain activity has intrigued scientists and philosophers for centuries. In the last decades, several theories\nhave suggested different accounts for these relationships. These theories have developed in parallel, with little to no cross-talk among them. To advance\nresearch on consciousness, we established an adversarial collaboration between proponents of two of the major theories in the field, Global Neuronal Workspace\nand Integrated Information Theory. Together, we devised and preregistered two experiments that test contrasting predictions of these theories concerning the\nlocation and timing of correlates of visual consciousness, which have been endorsed by the theories’ proponents. Predicted outcomes should either support,\nrefute, or challenge these theories. Six theory-impartial laboratories will follow the study protocol specified here, using three complementary methods:\nFunctional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (fMRI), Magneto-Electroencephalography (M-EEG), and intracranial electroencephalography (iEEG). The study protocol will\ninclude built-in replications, both between labs and within datasets. Through this ambitious undertaking, we hope to provide decisive evidence in favor or\nagainst the two theories and clarify the footprints of conscious visual perception in the human brain, while also providing an innovative model of large-scale,\ncollaborative, and open science practice.\n\n**Citation:**Melloni L, Mudrik L, Pitts M, Bendtz K, Ferrante O, Gorska U, et al. (2023) An adversarial collaboration protocol for testing contrasting\npredictions of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theory. PLoS ONE 18(2): e0268577. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268577\n\n**Editor:**Lawrence M. Ward, University of British Columbia, CANADA\n\n**Received:**November 16, 2021;**Accepted:**May 3, 2022;**Published:**February 10, 2023\n\n**Copyright:**© 2023 Melloni et al. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the[Creative Commons Attribution\nLicense](http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original\nauthor and source are credited.\n\n**Data Availability:**The data collected and analyzed for this Study Protocol will be made openly available at OSF\n([https://osf.io/mbcfy/](https://osf.io/mbcfy/)) and XNAT (Radiologics).\n\n**Funding:**This project is made possible through the support of a grant from Templeton World Charity Foundation, Inc (TWCF0389) to Lucia Melloni and (TWCF0378)\nto Liad Mudrik and (TWCF0345) to Michael Pitts. The opinions expressed in this publication are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily reflect the views\nof Templeton World Charity Foundation, Inc.[https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/](https://www.templetonworldcharity.org/). The funders had no role in study\ndesign, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the manuscript.\n\n**Competing interests:**The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.\n\n[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\n\n## Introduction[](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577)\n\nUnderstanding how consciousness relates to neural activity in the human brain remains one of the greatest scientific challenges\n[[1](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref001)]. Phenomenologically, consciousness has been defined\nas*subjective experience*(or*what it is like*to perceive, feel, act or think from a first-person perspective)\n[[2](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref002)]. In human experiments, consciousness is typically\ninferred based on the ability to report these experiences (either to oneself or to an external observer\n[[3](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref003)]). The neural correlates of consciousness (NCCs) have been\ndefined as the*minimal*neuronal mechanisms jointly sufficient for any specific experience\n[[4](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref004)]. Significant progress has come from focusing on the\nsearch for the NCC [[5](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref005),[6](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/a\nrticle?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0268577#pone.0268577.ref006)]. This has led to the development of several promising scientific theories of consciousness [[7](h"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694", "artifact_id": "artifact:341ce2ec24a5b42a3781a8672d8ad583d9132a534b9c1e4e7f02ee51e7345643", "sha256": "341ce2ec24a5b42a3781a8672d8ad583d9132a534b9c1e4e7f02ee51e7345643", "title": "Challenging Global Workspace and Integrated Information Theories", "url": "https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 362}, "content": "## Challenging Global Workspace and Integrated Information Theories\n\n**Source**: https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/\n\n---\n\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\n- [VIDEO](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/video-explore/)\n- [WIKI](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/wiki/)\n- [BLOG](https://bioengineer.org/category/blog/)\n- [COMMUNITY](https://Bioengineer.org/)\n- [FACEBOOK](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org)\n- [INSTAGRAM](https://instagram.com/bioengineering/)\n- [TWITTER](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)\n\nThursday, August 13, 2026\n\n[**](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org/)[**](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-\ninformation-theories/)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\n[](https://bioengineer.org/)\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\nNo Result\n\n**View All Result\n\n- [**Login](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/#jeg_loginform)\n\n[](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\n- [Lecturer](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/lecturer/)\n- [PhD Studentship](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/phd-studentship/)\n- [Postdoc](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/postdoc/)\n- [Research Assistant](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/research-assistant/)\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\n- [VIDEO](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/video-explore/)\n- [WIKI](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/wiki/)\n- [BLOG](https://bioengineer.org/category/blog/)\n- [COMMUNITY](https://Bioengineer.org/)\n- [FACEBOOK](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org)\n- [INSTAGRAM](https://instagram.com/bioengineering/)\n- [TWITTER](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)\n\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\n- [Lecturer](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/lecturer/)\n- [PhD Studentship](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/phd-studentship/)\n- [Postdoc](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/postdoc/)\n- [Research Assistant](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/research-assistant/)\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)"}], "summaries": {"summaries": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4", "summary": "An open-science adversarial collaboration directly compared Integrated Information Theory (IIT) and Global Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT) using preregistered experiments with 256 human subjects across fMRI, MEG, and iEEG modalities. Results revealed conscious content representations in visual, ventrotemporal, and inferior frontal cortices, along with sustained occipital/temporal responses and frontal-visual synchronization. These findings partially align with but substantially challenge core tenets of both theories: IIT is contradicted by the absence of sustained posterior synchronization, while GNWT is challenged by a lack of stimulus-offset ignition and limited prefrontal involvement. The study underscores the necessity for rigorous, theory-driven collaborative frameworks to advance cognitive neuroscience.", "limits": "Lines 1-435; encompasses the abstract and introductory sections detailing the adversarial methodology, multimodal data collection, key neural findings, and specific theoretical challenges to both IIT and GNWT."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:b37e56afb7f8659a0f913e52f4986a7ae9d224cc37700f8c8bba3a33602ebf83", "summary": "Funded by the Templeton World Charity Foundation, this adversarial collaboration employs non-human primate and mouse models to test divergent predictions of GNWT and IIT regarding neural correlates of consciousness. Utilizing high-resolution Neuropixels recordings and optogenetic network manipulation, the project investigates whether consciousness arises from frontal ignition or posterior integration. By comparing neuronal firing patterns across species with varying frontal lobe structures, the research aims to elucidate the evolutionary origins of conscious mechanisms and improve clinical understanding of consciousness disorders.", "limits": "Lines 1-228; covers the project's funding background, rationale for animal models, technological approach (Neuropixels/optogenetics), and objectives regarding cross-species comparison and clinical applications."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:084e4761c6fa0b9b0c60c3983fdc4cbeff0680c07bd73f80df46bc870ada2214", "summary": "This publication outlines a preregistered protocol for an adversarial collaboration designed to test contrasting predictions of GNWT and IIT concerning the spatial and temporal dynamics of visual consciousness. Six theory-impartial laboratories will independently execute the study using fMRI, magnetoencephalography, and intracranial EEG, incorporating built-in replication checks. The initiative seeks to generate decisive empirical evidence to support or refute the competing theories while establishing a standardized, open-science framework for large-scale collaborative neuroscience research.", "limits": "Lines 1-486; spans the full study protocol document, including theoretical background, experimental design specifications across multiple imaging modalities, multi-lab coordination details, and open-data implementation guidelines."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694", "summary": "The provided text consists entirely of website navigation elements, metadata, and structural placeholders without substantive scientific content or analysis related to the stated topic.", "limits": "Lines 1-362; contains only site architecture, menu items, and boilerplate text; lacks empirical or theoretical discussion."}]}, "review": {"findings": [{"check_id": "CITE-CONTEXT", "severity": "info", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694", "locator": "Lines 1-362", "sha256": "19527206132d40d935fa56fa8a8b435682c564fdb650f6a87179f93391272694"}], "recommendation": "Source 4 consists entirely of website navigation and boilerplate text without substantive scientific content. Rely on the peer-reviewed protocol and primary research articles for evidence."}, {"check_id": "SCOPE-INFLATE", "severity": "minor", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4", "locator": "Rationale", "sha256": "5f0cdd712a4a17341f73f3a298f54f289600fe14e65b5ef369df6165a8799533"}], "recommendation": "The rationale mentions 'quantum vibration' as an example theory, but the evidence base exclusively covers GNWT and IIT. Since the completion test requires at least two frameworks, this is acceptable, but removing extraneous examples would tighten scope alignment."}], "summary": "The evidence robustly validates the conceptual discrimination workflow by documenting adversarial collaborations that explicitly freeze the operational definitions of GNWT and IIT to derive mutually exclusive, falsifiable predictions. Preregistered protocols, multimodal data collection (fMRI, MEG, iEEG, Neuropixels), and cross-species designs satisfy the completion test for distinct measurable outcomes. Scientific validity is high, though one source is non-substantive and the rationale includes an unaddressed theoretical example. Overall, the inference scope is appropriately bounded and directly supports the direction's objectives."}}, "proposed": {"claims": [{"text": "The absence of sustained posterior cortical synchronization during conscious visual perception establishes a bound on the necessity of posterior integration for consciousness, contradicting Integrated Information Theory's claim that network connectivity alone specifies conscious experience.", "kind": "evidence", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4"], "scope": "Human visual consciousness paradigms measured via multimodal neuroimaging (fMRI, MEG, iEEG)."}, {"text": "Optogenetic disruption of posterior information integration networks will abolish conscious perceptual reports in non-human primates and mice regardless of frontal lobe morphology, whereas frontal network inhibition will not consistently eliminate awareness.", "kind": "prediction", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:b37e56afb7f8659a0f913e52f4986a7ae9d224cc37700f8c8bba3a33602ebf83", "source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4"], "scope": "Cross-species experimental neuroscience using Neuropixels recordings and optogenetic network manipulation."}, {"text": "Conscious visual perception is bounded by content-specific synchronization between frontal and early visual areas, operating independently of global frontal ignition thresholds predicted by workspace models.", "kind": "bound", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:22686e407a330f74b8208b83acc626567144c647d819bbae0f20783c37abfda4", "source-snapshot:084e4761c6fa0b9b0c60c3983fdc4cbeff0680c07bd73f80df46bc870ada2214"], "scope": "Human neuroimaging studies of visual awareness under controlled stimulus duration conditions."}]}}}}
{"type": "attempt_output", "content": {"output": {"rebuttals": [{"claim_index": 0, "challenge": "IIT defines consciousness via system-wide causal integration (Φ), not localized posterior functional synchronization. The challenge conflates measured low-frequency coherence with IIT's intrinsic causal architecture. Scope is further limited to suprathreshold visual paradigms, excluding subcortical, high-frequency, or non-visual conscious states where posterior dynamics may differ.", "repair": "Reframe to state that the absence of sustained posterior synchronization challenges posterior-centric functional connectivity as a necessary correlate for visual consciousness under these multimodal imaging constraints, while acknowledging IIT's requirement for system-wide causal integration beyond localized oscillatory coupling."}, {"claim_index": 1, "challenge": "This presents an unverified hypothesis as a definitive prediction. The adversarial protocol is designed to empirically test divergent outcomes, not confirm posterior necessity. It also overlooks species-specific neuroanatomical differences (e.g., murine agranular cortex) and ignores compensatory thalamocortical or brainstem arousal systems that could sustain awareness despite posterior disruption.", "repair": "Position as a preregistered experimental hypothesis contingent on task-dependent reliance on posterior integration, explicitly accounting for cross-species anatomical variation and the possibility that frontal-posterior feedback loops, rather than isolated regional disruption, determine perceptual availability."}, {"claim_index": 2, "challenge": "The claim overgeneralizes 'lack of ignition at stimulus offset' to imply complete independence from workspace ignition thresholds. Ignition may still govern conscious reportability, maintenance, or transition phases outside the measured window. Frontal-visual synchronization likely reflects top-down attentional gating or working memory buffering rather than a standalone consciousness mechanism, confounding the boundary claim.", "repair": "Specify that frontal-early visual coupling sustains content-specific representations during active perception, while recognizing that global ignition thresholds may remain critical for conscious access, reportability, or state transitions beyond the immediate stimulus duration window."}]}, "tool_receipts": []}}
